systemE on Debian and Ubuntu
============================

systemE replaces systemd. Installing it removes the distribution's systemd,
systemd-sysv, udev, libsystemd0, libudev1 and related packages, and provides
the same names, so everything that depends on them stays installed.

Before you reboot
-----------------

systemE does not ship systemd-networkd or systemd-resolved. On Ubuntu Server,
netplan normally renders to systemd-networkd, so a machine set up that way
comes back with no network. Install and configure NetworkManager, ifupdown or
connman first, and point /etc/resolv.conf at a resolver that exists.

logind needs a system bus, which is why systeme depends on dbus.

Going back
----------

    apt install systemd systemd-sysv udev libsystemd0 libudev1

apt removes systeme in the same transaction, the same way it was put in.

If the machine does not boot, add init=/bin/sh to the kernel command line from
the boot loader to get a shell.
